#!/usr/bin/env python3
# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
"""rpi-audiod: keeps the gadget fed and bridges USB audio and MIDI devices to it (docs/PLAN.md).

While the host streams both ways, it runs jackd on the gadget (mode A) and, for every USB
audio card, a zita-a2j / zita-j2a bridge per direction, connected to that device's gadget
channels. While the host only records from the Pi, it plays silence instead: some hosts
drop the gadget when nothing plays into it (docs/hw/2026-10-29-f1-force.md). When the
gadget has USB MIDI ports, every MIDI port of a USB device gets one of them, and the
control port takes the commands below from the host too (docs/MIDI.md).

  rpi-audiod [run]                            the service (rpi-audiod.service)
  rpi-audiod status                           what the service is doing
  rpi-audiod assign IDENTITY [--in N] [--out N] [--midi K]   pin a device's gadget channels or MIDI port
  rpi-audiod mute IDENTITY in|out on|off      cut or restore a device's audio routes
  rpi-audiod volume IDENTITY in|out PERCENT   set a device's own capture or playback level

Gadget channels are sticky per device identity (/var/lib/rpi-audio/slots.json). A full-speed
device is only bridged while its isochronous load fits [usb] fs_budget on its bus. Settings:
/etc/rpi-audio/rpi-audio.toml, then /etc/rpi-audio/rpi-audio.d/*.toml; optional per-device
profiles in /etc/rpi-audio/devices.d/*.toml (config/devices.d/); the gadget's own
come from the GADGET_* environment, or from gadget.env.
"""

